<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN" "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
A Kubernetes <code>Secret</code> is the intended place for credentials: its contents are
stored separately from the objects that reference them, can be encrypted at rest, and can
be restricted with their own RBAC rules. A custom resource is not. Anyone with
<code>get</code> or <code>list</code> on the custom resource type — and anyone reading
<code>kubectl get -o yaml</code> output, a GitOps mirror of cluster state, or an audit log
entry that captured the object — sees every field of it.
</p>

<p>
This query reports a value read out of the <code>Data</code> or <code>StringData</code> map
of a <code>k8s.io/api/core/v1.Secret</code> that reaches, through taint, a string-typed
field of a type the operator registered as a custom resource with
<code>sigs.k8s.io/controller-runtime/pkg/scheme</code>. The typical shape is an operator
that resolves a <code>SecretKeySelector</code> into the plaintext value and then writes that
value back into the spec or status of the resource it is reconciling, so that the secret
ends up copied into an object with much weaker access control than the
<code>Secret</code> it came from.
</p>

<p>
The result is a path from the read of the secret to the write into the custom resource, so
the intermediate assignments can be inspected to judge whether the value really is
sensitive.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Keep the plaintext out of the custom resource. Hold the resolved value in a local variable
for as long as the reconcile needs it, and store only a reference — the
<code>SecretKeySelector</code> naming the <code>Secret</code> and key — in the resource
itself.
</p>

<p>
Where the resource has to record something about the secret so that a change can be
detected, record a non-reversible summary rather than the value: a hash, the
<code>resourceVersion</code> of the <code>Secret</code>, or a generation counter.
</p>

<p>
If a field genuinely must carry credential material, put it in a <code>Secret</code> the
operator creates and owns, and reference that from the custom resource.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the operator reads a token out of a <code>Secret</code> and
assigns it to a field of the custom resource it is reconciling. The token is now visible to
every reader of the custom resource.
</p>

<sample language="go">
func (r *WidgetReconciler) reconcile(ctx context.Context, widget *v1alpha1.Widget) error {
	var secret corev1.Secret
	if err := r.Get(ctx, key, &amp;secret); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	// BAD: the plaintext token is copied into the custom resource.
	widget.Status.Token = string(secret.Data["token"])

	return r.Status().Update(ctx, widget)
}
</sample>

<p>
In the corrected version the plaintext stays in a local variable, and the custom resource
records only which <code>Secret</code> was used and a digest of the value.
</p>

<sample language="go">
func (r *WidgetReconciler) reconcile(ctx context.Context, widget *v1alpha1.Widget) error {
	var secret corev1.Secret
	if err := r.Get(ctx, key, &amp;secret); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	token := string(secret.Data["token"])
	if err := r.callAPI(ctx, token); err != nil {
		return err
	}

	// GOOD: only a reference and a digest are persisted.
	widget.Status.TokenSecretRef = corev1.SecretKeySelector{
		LocalObjectReference: corev1.LocalObjectReference{Name: secret.Name},
		Key:                  "token",
	}
	widget.Status.TokenHash = fmt.Sprintf("%x", sha256.Sum256([]byte(token)))

	return r.Status().Update(ctx, widget)
}
</sample>
</example>

